## The gain from fixed weights depends on their use

We predicted that a fixed-weight datapath is {{ sig(PRD.P15.central) }}x smaller and uses
{{ sig(PRD.P16.central) }}x less energy. The synthesis shows that the result depends on the use of the weights.

| how the fixed weights reach the lanes | reloadable / fixed, area | reloadable / fixed, energy |
|---|---|---|
| **a lane always sees the same weight** (the same 16 weights in every block of the row, so the constants fold into the multipliers) | **{{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}** ({{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.min, 2) }} to {{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.max, 2) }}, n={{ k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.n }}) | **{{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}** ({{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.min, 2) }} to {{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.max, 2) }}) |
| **a whole row of {{ n(fix.weights_in_module) }} weights time-shared through 16 lanes** (the weights become a table in logic) | {{ n(Z.ratios_reloadable_over_fixed.cell_area.median, 2) }} | {{ n(Z.ratios_reloadable_over_fixed.energy_per_block.median, 2) }} |

- When every lane has its own fixed weight, the block is {{ n(k1.area_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}x smaller and
  uses {{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }}x less energy. The multipliers fold away. The adder tree
  and the 32-bit accumulator stay, and they are most of the block. This is the comparison that the
  predictions meant. Area was {{ PRD.P15.verdict }} the predicted range, and energy was {{ PRD.P16.verdict }} it.

  A first version of this test used rows one block long. Synthesis then also removed the
  accumulator and showed a larger decrease. That result stays in the JSON with a warning, and we do
  not use it.
- When the fixed weights are time-shared, the fixed module is *larger* than the reloadable module.
  The fixed module contains its weights, and the reloadable module does not. As storage, the fixed
  module is dense: {{ n(fix.area_per_weight_um2, 2) }} µm² per hard-wired weight in standard cells, with the datapath.
  For scale, the one shipped fixed-weight chip holds 8B weights on 815 mm². That is about
  {{ n(815e6 / 8e9, 2) }} µm² per weight on an older process with custom cells (taalas-hc1).

Thus a fixed weight in a lane saves little in that lane. The reloadable design is still larger, for
two reasons:

- An SRAM bit is larger than a mask-programmed bit.
- S has a multiplier lane on every bank, which is {{ pct(I.designs.s.area.mac_lanes / I.designs.s.area.silicon_mm2) }} of its silicon. The density figure of the
  fixed design comes from one shipped chip with its arithmetic folded in.

Public data does not show how much of the difference comes from reloadability and how much comes
from the design of that chip.

![synthesized block](../results/fig_silicon.png)

We measured the middle bar (one weight per lane) on its own one-block workload. On that same
workload, the reloadable block uses {{ n(k1.reloadable_energy_per_block_pj_same_workload.median, 2) }} pJ. That value gives the
{{ n(k1.energy_ratio_reloadable_over_fixed.median, 2) }} in the table.

## From the open kit to an N4P-class process

ASAP7 is a predictive kit. Its numbers are reliable relative to one another, but not as predictions
of a foundry process. Three factors convert them, and all three are in the uncertainty sweep.

| | on ASAP7 | factor | N4P-class value used | assumed before synthesis | new / old |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| area per NVFP4 lane | {{ n(SS.asap7.area_per_mac_lane_um2, 1) }} µm² of cells | × {{ SS.factors.area }} (cell pitches; asap7-scaling), ÷ {{ SS.factors.utilization }} placement utilization (assumption) | {{ n(SS.n4p.um2_per_mac_fp4, 1) }} µm² | {{ n(SS.assumed_before.um2_per_mac_fp4, 0) }} µm² | {{ x(SS.measured_over_assumed.area) }} |
| energy per NVFP4 operation | {{ n(SS.asap7.energy_per_mac_pj / 2, 3) }} pJ | × {{ SS.factors.energy }} (N5 to N4P; n4p) | {{ n(SS.n4p.pj_per_flop_fp4, 3) }} pJ | {{ n(SS.assumed_before.pj_per_flop_fp4, 2) }} pJ | {{ x(SS.measured_over_assumed.energy, 2) }} |
| clock | {{ n(SS.asap7.clock_mhz, 0) }} MHz, timing closed | × {{ SS.factors.speed }} (N5 to N4P; n4p) | {{ n(SS.n4p.f_clk / 1e6, 0) }} MHz | {{ n(SS.assumed_before.f_clk / 1e6, 0) }} MHz | {{ x(SS.measured_over_assumed.clock, 2) }} |

We apply only the published N5-to-N4P step to speed and energy. Apart from that step, we treat
ASAP7 as N5-class, which is conservative. We built only the NVFP4 lane. The MXFP8 and BF16 lanes
take the same area correction and keep their assumed energy.

